---
intent: >
  Refactor aide_upgrade from a two-phase dry-run/confirm executor returning
  prose into an agent-driven comparator returning structured JSON grouped by
  category. The tool compares and reports; the agent presents per-category
  results and applies only what the user confirms. MCP config changes become
  prescriptions. Malformed JSON is reported as a status, never clobbered.
---

## Plan

### Phase 1 — Shared types (coordinate with init plan)

Steps 1.1 through 1.1 are defined in the init plan and executed there first.
The `McpPrescription` type in `src/types/index.ts` is shared by both tools.
This plan depends on that type being present.

- [x] **1.1 Define `UpgradeCategory` string literal union in `src/types/index.ts`.**
  Values: `"pointer-stub"`, `"methodology-docs"`, `"version-metadata"`,
  `"commands"`, `"agents"`, `"skills"`, `"mcp"`, `"ide"`. These are the
  per-category groups the agent presents to the user. The user confirms or
  declines each category independently.

- [x] **1.2 Define `UpgradeFileStatus` string literal union in `src/types/index.ts`.**
  Values: `"matches"`, `"differs"`, `"missing"`, `"malformed"`. These replace
  the current `UpgradeStatus` which mixed dry-run and write-phase statuses
  (`"would update"`, `"updated"`, etc.). The tool is now read-only — it
  only reports comparison results. `"malformed"` is for MCP config only.

- [x] **1.3 Define `UpgradeFileResult` interface in `src/types/index.ts`.**
  Fields: `name: string`, `filePath: string`, `status: UpgradeFileStatus`,
  `category: UpgradeCategory`, `canonicalContent?: string` (the content to
  write if the agent applies — present for `differs` and `missing`),
  `prescription?: McpPrescription` (for MCP category only).

- [x] **1.4 Define `UpgradeCategoryResult` interface in `src/types/index.ts`.**
  Fields: `category: UpgradeCategory`, `files: UpgradeFileResult[]`,
  `summary: { total: number; differs: number; missing: number; matches: number }`.
  The agent uses the summary to decide whether to present the category at all
  (skip categories where everything matches).

- [x] **1.5 Define `UpgradeResult` interface in `src/types/index.ts`.**
  Fields: `framework: FrameworkType`, `categories: UpgradeCategoryResult[]`.
  This is the structured JSON the tool returns. No prose, no formatting.

- [x] **1.6 Remove old `UpgradeStatus` and `UpgradeStepResult` from
  `src/types/index.ts`.**
  They are replaced by `UpgradeFileStatus` and `UpgradeFileResult`. Removal
  here forces all consumers to update.

### Phase 2 — Refactor compareFile to return structured data

- [x] **2.1 Refactor `compareFile/index.ts` to return `UpgradeFileStatus`.**
  Remove the `write` parameter entirely — the tool never writes. The function
  reads the host file and compares against canonical content. Returns
  `"matches"`, `"differs"`, or `"missing"`. No `mkdir`, no `writeFile`.
  Signature becomes: `(hostPath: string, canonicalContent: string) =>
  Promise<UpgradeFileStatus>`.

### Phase 3 — Refactor spliceStub to return structured data

- [x] **3.1 Refactor `spliceStub/index.ts` to return `UpgradeFileResult`.**
  Remove the `write` parameter. The function reads the config file, locates
  the marker-bounded region, compares the current stub against canonical, and
  returns an `UpgradeFileResult` with status `matches`, `differs`, or
  `missing`. For `differs` and `missing`, include `canonicalContent` with the
  full config file content after splicing (so the agent can write it directly).
  Category is `"pointer-stub"`.

### Phase 4 — Refactor MCP comparison

- [x] **4.1 Extract `checkMcpConfig` from the upgrade orchestrator into
  `src/tools/upgrade/checkMcpConfig/index.ts`.**
  Currently this is an inline function in the orchestrator. Extract it to its
  own helper following the orchestrator/helper pattern. It returns an
  `UpgradeFileResult` with category `"mcp"`. When the config is malformed
  JSON, return status `"malformed"` (no prescription, no canonicalContent).
  When the aide entry differs from canonical, return status `"differs"` with
  the `McpPrescription`. When it matches, return `"matches"`. When the file
  is missing, return `"missing"` with the prescription. Never write.

### Phase 5 — Refactor IDE comparison

- [x] **5.1 Extract `checkZedConfig` and `checkVscodeExtension` into
  `src/tools/upgrade/checkIdeConfig/index.ts`.**
  Currently these are inline functions in the orchestrator. Extract them as
  named exports in a new helper. Each returns `UpgradeFileResult` with
  category `"ide"`. Zed: compare settings file for `*.aide` entry. VS Code:
  check extension list. Neither writes. Malformed Zed settings return
  `"malformed"` status.

### Phase 6 — Rewrite the upgrade orchestrator

- [x] **6.1 Rewrite `src/tools/upgrade/index.ts` to return `UpgradeResult`.**
  The orchestrator calls `detectFramework`, then runs comparison for each
  category: pointer stub (via `spliceStub`), methodology docs (via
  `compareFile` loop), version metadata (via `compareFile` on
  `buildVersionsMeta` output), commands (via `compareFile` loop), agents
  (via `compareFile` loop), skills (via `compareFile` loop), MCP config
  (via `checkMcpConfig`), IDE config (via `checkIdeConfig`). Group results
  into `UpgradeCategoryResult` objects with computed summaries. Return the
  `UpgradeResult`. No writing, no prose formatting.

- [x] **6.2 Update `UpgradeInput` Zod schema.**
  Remove `confirm` — there is no confirm phase in the tool anymore; the agent
  applies changes directly. Remove `skipIde` — the agent handles per-category
  decisions. Keep `framework` (optional override) and `path` (optional
  project root).

- [x] **6.3 Update the MCP server handler in `src/index.ts` for aide_upgrade.**
  Change the handler to return the `UpgradeResult` as JSON via
  `JSON.stringify`. Update the tool description to reflect the new
  structured-response, per-category contract. Update the `inputSchema` to
  match the simplified `UpgradeInput`.

### Phase 7 — Tests

- [x] **7.1 Rewrite `src/tools/upgrade/index.test.ts`.**
  The test now asserts on the structured `UpgradeResult` object, not prose
  strings. Key test cases from the spec:
  - All matching: every category summary has `differs: 0, missing: 0`.
  - Some drifted: methodology docs category has `differs > 0`, other
    categories match. Verify `canonicalContent` is populated on differing
    files.
  - MCP config malformed: mcp category file has status `"malformed"`, no
    prescription.
  - MCP config differs: mcp category file has status `"differs"` with
    prescription containing the canonical aide server entry.
  - Missing files: status is `"missing"` with `canonicalContent` populated.
  - Categories are correctly grouped — each file lands in the right category.
  - Framework override works.

- [x] **7.2 Update `compareFile/index.test.ts`.**
  Remove all test cases for `write: true`. Add test for `"missing"` status
  (file does not exist). Verify no filesystem writes occur.

- [x] **7.3 Update `spliceStub` tests.**
  Remove write-phase assertions. Assert on `UpgradeFileResult` structure
  with `canonicalContent` for the differs/missing cases.

- [x] **7.4 Write `checkMcpConfig/index.test.ts`.**
  New helper needs tests: malformed JSON returns `"malformed"`, matching
  aide entry returns `"matches"`, differing entry returns `"differs"` with
  prescription, missing file returns `"missing"` with prescription, legacy
  `aidemd-mcp` key is detected as `"differs"`.

- [x] **7.5 Write `checkIdeConfig/index.test.ts`.**
  Zed: exists with `*.aide` returns `"matches"`, exists without returns
  `"differs"`, missing file returns `"missing"`. VS Code: extension
  installed returns `"matches"`, not installed returns `"differs"`.

- [x] **7.6 Run `vitest run` and fix any remaining type or assertion errors.**

## Decisions

**1. Tool returns structured JSON grouped by category, not prose.**
The spec is explicit: "The tool always returns a structured JSON response
— never a pre-formatted string — containing per-file comparison results
grouped by category." The current `Promise<string>` return becomes
`Promise<UpgradeResult>`. Traced to: spec `outcomes.desired` first bullet,
spec `outcomes.undesired` second bullet.

**2. Remove `confirm` parameter entirely.**
The current two-phase dry-run/confirm model is replaced by the agent-driven
model where the tool only compares and the agent applies. There is no
`confirm: true` path in the tool. The agent reads the structured results,
presents per-category, and writes files directly using the `canonicalContent`
provided. Traced to: spec Strategy "The tool is a comparator; the agent is
the executor."

**3. Per-category grouping with summaries.**
The spec requires "per-category control replaces all-or-nothing confirm."
Each `UpgradeCategoryResult` includes a summary so the agent can skip
all-matching categories and only present drifted ones to the user. The
user confirms per-category, not per-file (spec: "Within a category, all
files are updated together — no per-file opt-out").

**4. `canonicalContent` on differs/missing results.**
The agent needs the content to write without calling back to the tool.
For `differs` and `missing` files, the result includes the full canonical
content. For `matches`, it is omitted (no write needed). This mirrors the
init plan's `content` field pattern. The agent writes the file at the
`filePath` with the `canonicalContent` bytes.

**5. `"malformed"` as an explicit file status.**
The spec says "When the existing MCP config is malformed JSON, the tool
reports 'malformed' status instead of clobbering." This is a first-class
status value, not a flag, because it replaces what was previously a silent
overwrite. The agent handles the user conversation when it sees this status.
Traced to: spec `outcomes.desired` fourth bullet.

**6. Extract inline helpers into proper subfolders.**
The current upgrade orchestrator has `checkMcpConfig`, `checkZedConfig`, and
`checkVscodeExtension` as inline functions. Progressive disclosure requires
helpers in subfolders with `index.ts` and default exports. Extracting them
makes the folder tree readable (Tier 1) and each helper independently
testable. Playbook convention: orchestrator/helper pattern
(progressive-disclosure.md, folder-structure.md).

**7. Shared `McpPrescription` type with init.**
Both plans use the same prescription type defined in `src/types/index.ts`.
This is the coordination point: the agent's MCP merge logic is identical
whether it came from init or upgrade. One type, one merge path.

**8. `compareFile` loses its write parameter.**
The function was doing double duty: comparison and conditional write. The
spec's agent-driven model means the tool never writes. Removing the `write`
parameter makes the function pure (read two things, compare, return status).
This simplifies testing and eliminates the possibility of accidental writes.

**9. `spliceStub` returns the full spliced content, not just a status.**
The agent needs to write the config file with the stub spliced in. Computing
the spliced content requires marker detection logic that lives in
`spliceStub`. Rather than duplicate that logic in the agent, `spliceStub`
returns the full post-splice content as `canonicalContent`. The agent
writes it as-is.
